<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="review-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">7543770</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2034004</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Tumour vasculature--a potential therapeutic target.</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Baillie</surname><given-names>C. T.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Winslet</surname><given-names>M. C.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Bradley</surname><given-names>N. J.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff>University Department of Surgery, Royal Free Hospital and School of Medicine, London, UK.</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>8</month><year>1995</year></pub-date><volume>72</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>257</fpage><lpage>267</lpage><abstract><p>The tumour vasculature is vital for the establishment, growth and metastasis of solid tumours. Its physiological properties limit the effectiveness of conventional anti-cancer strategies. Therapeutic approaches directed at the tumour vasculature are reviewed, suggesting the potential of anti-angiogenesis and the targeting of vascular proliferation antigens as cancer treatments.</p></abstract></article-meta></front><body><supplementary-material content-type="scanned-pages"><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00042-0005.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="257" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00042-0006.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="258" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00042-0007.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="259" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00042-0008.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="260" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00042-0009.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="261" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00042-0010.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="262" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00042-0011.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="263" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00042-0012.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="264" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00042-0013.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="265" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00042-0014.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="266" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00042-0015.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="267" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/></supplementary-material></body></article>